digraph ReasoningTree {
  rankdir="TB";
  "I1" [label="(0,0,0) If AI/ML models become larger/compute-hungry and hardware efficiency cannot keep up, then new computing architectures are needed to sustain progress and enable deployment under resource constraints."];
  "I2" [label="(0,0,0) If a research area lacks fair, objective, widely adopted benchmarks, then progress slows because solutions cannot be quantified, compared, or tracked over time."];
  "N1" [label="(1,0,0) Currently, AI/ML growth is producing increasingly large and complex models to improve accuracy and broaden use cases."];
  "N11" [label="(19,0,0) Currently, an outstanding benchmark challenge is lack of a formal definition of what counts as “neuromorphic” to benchmark, because approaches are diverse."];
  "N12" [label="(20,0,0) If a benchmark definition is closed/narrow, then it can impose narrow assumptions and unfairly exclude promising methods."];
  "N13" [label="(21,0,0) If lack of a formal definition is a challenge, then benchmarks should be inclusive and broadly applicable, allow flexible implementation, and focus on task capabilities and metrics such as temporal processing and efficiency."];
  "N15" [label="(23,0,0) Currently, neuromorphic research uses a wide array of different frameworks (e.g., neuroscientific exploration, automatic SNN training), creating implementation diversity."];
  "N16" [label="(24,0,0) Currently, implementation diversity reduces portability and standardization, which limits the ease of benchmark implementation."];
  "N17" [label="(25,0,0) If implementation diversity limits benchmark implementation, then benchmarks require common infrastructure/tooling that unites implementation and enables comparison."];
  "N2" [label="(2,0,0) Currently, model-computation demand is growing faster than hardware-efficiency gains from Moore/Dennard scaling, so existing techniques face a looming improvement limit."];
  "N27" [label="(36,0,0) Currently, neuromorphic hardware has not converged to a single commercial platform, so much research advances algorithms on conventional systems that may be suboptimal."];
  "N3" [label="(3,0,0) Currently, adapting these resource-intensive AI/ML methods to resource-constrained edge devices (tinyML/IoT) remains an open challenge."];
  "N4" [label="(4,0,0) Currently, there is intensified urgency to explore new resource-efficient and scalable computing architectures."];
  "N5" [label="(5,0,0) Currently, neuromorphic computing is a promising area to address these challenges by porting brain-inspired primitives and strategies into engineered devices and algorithms."];
  "N6" [label="(6,0,0) Currently, the brain is an exceptional model for scalable, energy-efficient, real-time embodied computation, so neuromorphic systems are critical for novel-architecture investigation."];
  "N7" [label="(15,0,0) Currently, neuromorphic progress is impeded because there are no fair, widely adopted objective metrics and benchmarks."];
  "N8" [label="(16,0,0) If benchmarks are absent, then the validity of neuromorphic solutions cannot be directly quantified, which hinders measuring technological advancement."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This paper motivates, develops, and evaluates NeuroBench as an open neuromorphic benchmarking suite with inclusive task-level benchmarking, common tooling, standardized performance and efficiency evaluation, and separate algorithm and system tracks to support fair comparison and algorithm-hardware co-design.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When a research field has growing computational demands, limited fair benchmarks, definitional diversity, implementation diversity, and non-standardized hardware, a useful benchmark suite should provide inclusive tasks, common tools, standardized metrics, and separated algorithm/system evaluation."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) Because implementation diversity limits benchmark implementation, neuromorphic benchmarking needs common infrastructure and tooling to unite implementations and enable comparison."];
  "R19" [label="(0,0,0) Because neuromorphic hardware platforms are not yet standardized, fair benchmarking should separate hardware-independent algorithm evaluation from hardware-dependent system evaluation."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) Because neuromorphic progress is impeded by the absence of fair, widely adopted objective metrics and benchmarks, standard rigorous benchmarking is needed to quantify, compare, and track progress."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Given the lack of a formal definition of what counts as neuromorphic, neuromorphic benchmarks should be inclusive, broadly applicable, flexible in implementation, and focused on task capabilities and efficiency-related metrics."];
  "N1" -> "I1" [label="deduction-case"];
  "N2" -> "I1" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "I1" -> "N4" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "N3" -> "N4" [label="deduction-case"];
  "N4" -> "N5" [label="abduction-phenomenon"];
  "N6" -> "N5" [label="abduction-knowledge"];
  "N8" -> "I2" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "N7" -> "I2" [label="deduction-case"];
  "N12" -> "N13" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "N11" -> "N13" [label="deduction-case"];
  "N16" -> "N17" [label="deduction-case"];
  "N15" -> "N17" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "I2" -> "R5" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "N7" -> "R5" [label="deduction-case"];
  "N13" -> "R7" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "N11" -> "R7" [label="deduction-case"];
  "N17" -> "R14" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "N16" -> "R14" [label="deduction-case"];
  "N27" -> "R19" [label="abduction-phenomenon"];
  "R5" -> "R19" [label="abduction-knowledge"];
  "N5" -> "NROOT" [label="induction-case"];
  "R14" -> "NROOT" [label="induction-case"];
  "R19" -> "NROOT" [label="induction-case"];
